{{- define "wake_blocked_tasks" -}}
Tasks are BLOCKED. Analyze and resolve immediately:

{{template "replacement_lineage_policy" .}}

1. Read blocked tasks from blackboard — understand blocked_reason, blocked_questions, and repair_request if present
2. Determine if blocker is:
   - approved-proof reference drift → read {{binaryName}} reaffirm-proof --help; orchestrator-only remedy, one transition only, no status change or approval.
   - Rejection RCA gate (`rejection_rca` without disposition) → run {{binaryName}} record-rejection-rca <task-id> --rca-file <file> --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json, then {{binaryName}} resume-rejection-rca <task-id> --disposition-file <file> --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json. Use unblock-task below per recorded restore_mode: assign requires --assign-to; claimable permits either form; none requires supersession, never unblock.
   - Structured repair_request present → verify the target state still needs repair, then complete the shared semantic gate below before applying a valid orchestrator-only operation
   - Missing dependency → create prerequisite task (run {{binaryName}} add-tasks --tasks-file <path.json> --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json NOW)
   - Runtime/environment failure or stale ownership → diagnose the host/session; narrow supported recovery, work preserved
   - Wrong or excessive dependency → state what each disputed edge supplies and what fails without it, then graph repair below; never delete all inherited edges (documentation can be a real provider)
   - Corrected inputs, valid task → unblock (--rebase-on when integration moved); run commands NOW
   - Genuine product ambiguity → assess-blocked with the minimal unresolved decision for the human, then route the answer to the source owner; not supersession by default
   - Unsupported technical assumption → the owning specialist investigates or proposes a feasible alternative; a product trade-off goes to the human
   - Blocked only on existing unfinished work (e.g. an earlier stage awaiting a later-stage correction) → route any correction to the source owner, then: edge allowed by direction (step 3) and acyclic → graph repair (retarget-dependency <task-id> <old> <old>,<new> or a complete-list repair), then unblock without --assign-to; direction forbids it → never a backward edge, hold with assess-blocked --awaits (step 5); edge would close a cycle → break the cycle first
   - Immutable reviewed ancestry cannot consume the correction → reviewed producer, normal generation, supported supersession, lineages preserved
   - Scope removed (Won't) → cancel-task for removed-only scope; reviewed replacement + supersede-task for mixed; corrective task for merged work; none proves frozen integration coverage
3. Before either stored apply-dependency-repair or direct retarget-dependency operation:
   a. Re-read every affected task and its planning/decomposition context.
   b. Verify provider-before-consumer direction for every proposed edge: a consumer may depend on its provider; a provider must not depend on its consumer.
   c. For any inverse edge, confirm the stored repair request or direct repair reason identifies the explicit relationship that independently requires the provider to depend on the consumer.
   d. Do not run either repair operation until this shared semantic gate passes.
4. For each blocked task, decide action:
   - To apply a stored multi-task or complete-list dependency repair:
     a. Re-read the blocked source task and confirm its request operation is `apply-dependency-repair` and its expected dependency lists still match current state.
     b. Run: {{binaryName}} apply-dependency-repair <blocked-task-id> --reason "Apply the stored dependency graph repair" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
     c. The batch commits atomically or not at all and clears the consumed request. The source task remains BLOCKED.
     d. Re-read the source via {{binaryName}} get <blocked-task-id> --json. Use `dependency_repair_receipt.affected_task_ids` to inspect the committed tasks and run the receipt's declared validation before unblocking or assessing the source.
   - To repair one direct edge on a non-terminal task without replacing either task:
     a. Re-read the task via {{binaryName}} get <task-id> --json to confirm it is still BLOCKED and still has the old depends_on edge.
     b. Verify the new dependency task(s) already exist and represent the intended blocker.
     c. The repair reason must identify the verified direction or name the explicit relationship that justifies an inverse edge.
     d. Run: {{binaryName}} retarget-dependency <task-id> <old-dep-id> <new-dep-id[,new-dep-id]> --reason "Correct dependency edge because <why>" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
     e. The task remains BLOCKED. Run the repair validation, then re-read {{binaryName}} get <task-id> --json as the canonical current view.
     f. If graph repair leaves a current blocker, reconcile its canonical metadata with: {{binaryName}} assess-blocked <task-id> --reason "<current blocker>" --question "<current question>" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json. Include every current question and, when needed, one complete replacement repair request.
     g. If graph repair resolves every blocker, use {{binaryName}} unblock-task; do not leave stale blocker metadata behind.
   - To resume after verified repair:
     a. Re-read the task via {{binaryName}} get <task-id> --json to confirm it is still BLOCKED.
     b. Verify the blocker is no longer true using current repair_request validation, consumed `dependency_repair_receipt.validation`, current worktree state, or another concrete check.
     c. If the task has a preserved worktree and integration moved while blocked, prefer: {{binaryName}} unblock-task <task-id> --rebase-on <integration-branch> --reason "Verified blocker is resolved" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
     d. Otherwise run: {{binaryName}} unblock-task <task-id> --reason "Verified blocker is resolved" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
     e. Add --assign-to <doer-agent-id> only when direct resume is required; no live process check is required for unblock assignment.
     f. A --rebase-on conflict remains BLOCKED with a fresh repair_request; do not treat it as submit/merge INTEGRATION_FAILED.
   - To supersede (create clean replacement):
   Example supersede workflow:
   a. Re-read the task via {{binaryName}} get <task-id> --json to confirm it is still BLOCKED.
      If status changed (e.g., task was claimed), skip — the task is being actively worked.
   b. Write a tasks JSON file as a bare array — NOT a wrapper object:
      [{
        "id": "<new-task-id>",
        "desc": "<short task description>",
        "spec": "<spec path>",
        "done": "<falsifiable completion condition>",
        "scope": "<allowed files or behavior>",
        "priority": 1,
        "depends": [],
        "type": "coding",
        "role_pair": "coding-pair"
      }]
   c. Run: {{binaryName}} add-tasks --tasks-file <path.json> --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json (create replacement tasks)
   d. Run: {{binaryName}} supersede-task <task-id> <new-id-1>,<new-id-2> --reason "Split due to complexity" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
   e. Run: {{binaryName}} wt-delete <task-id> --json (if worktree exists)
5. After analyzing each blocked task:
   - If you resolved it (unblocked or superseded): name the closure evidence — the intended consumer is eligible or progresses beyond the old failure point; a new task ID or transition alone is not closure
   - If you CANNOT resolve it (genuine spec ambiguity, external dependency) and canonical blocker metadata is already current: run note-only {{binaryName}} assess-blocked to record your assessment
     Run: {{binaryName}} assess-blocked <task-id> --note "<why it cannot be resolved now>" --agent-id {{.AgentID}} --json
     This history-only form prevents re-waking without replacing canonical blocker fields. The task will trigger again only when new information arrives (dependency changes, human notes).
     If the hold waits on specific unfinished tasks, add --awaits <id>[,<id>] naming all of them: it wakes once all merge or one fails. Later assessments keep the set minus merged tasks; --clear-awaits drops it, e.g. to wake on a plan's generated children. If an awaiting task wakes with no dependency change or human note, repeat the same --awaits: a rejection names the cycle, settled or failed task.
6. Execute all state-modifying commands in this session. Do NOT defer.

{{template "delivery_report" .}}

Do NOT call {{binaryName}} sprint-checkpoint. If you cannot resolve a blocker, assess it and exit. Other tasks must continue uninterrupted.
{{- end -}}
